---
// THE AUDIT TRAIL. Everything a skeptic needs, and nothing a buyer has to read first.
//
// These numbers used to sit on /capabilities, where they buried the answer the reader came for
// under two denominator paragraphs, a build stamp and a 24% headline that needs three
// paragraphs of context to be fair. Splitting them is not hiding them: /capabilities links here
// directly, and every figure still names the command that produced it.
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import data from '../data/capabilities.json';

const title = 'Receipts — every number, and the command that produced it';
const description =
  'The conformance score, the classification figures, and the exact capability families behind fidelic.dev/capabilities. Generated, with the denominator stated for every fraction.';
---

<Base title={title} description={description}>
  <article class="prose">
    <p class="back"><a href="/capabilities">← What Fidelic can run</a></p>
    <h1>Receipts</h1>

    <p>
      Every figure here is produced by running something, and each block names what. If a number
      on this site is wrong, the machine is wrong — which is the only failure mode worth having,
      because it is the one a test can catch.
    </p>

    <p class="stamp">
      Generated {data.generated} from build <code>{data.build}</code>, against emulator image
      <code>{data.imageRef}</code> reporting <code>{data.imageVersion}</code>. Shipped tier:
      <code>{data.shippedTier}</code>.
    </p>

    {/* THE HOMEPAGE'S OLD PROOF BLOCK, MOVED HERE WHOLE.
        It was three commands deep on a page whose job is to make one claim land, and it pushed
        everything that explains it further down. Here it is the point: this page's readers came
        for raw output, so nothing is summarised and the long lines scroll rather than wrap.

        VERBATIM, and re-verified line by line against fidelic/emulator:4ea9d49 on 2026-09-19
        against NPSP @ 19646faa, force-app subtree — not merely carried across from the old page.
        The coverage numbers, the ladder line, the boot line and the fault envelope were each
        re-run and compared before this section shipped.

        MOUNT force-app, NOT THE REPO ROOT. The first version of this block mounted ./npsp while
        showing output captured from ./npsp/force-app, so the commands and the output disagreed
        and nobody could tell: the repo root carries 9 more classes that live outside the
        deployable source and parse with errors, giving 1,514 classes and "3 with ERROR nodes"
        instead of 1,503 and zero.

        The build stamp on the boot line is elided with … because it changes every release. The
        14.1s is OURS — sfemu prints no boot duration — so it is a # comment, measured on the
        production box, a Hetzner CPX21 with 3 vCPU. */}
    <h2>End to end, in three commands</h2>
    <p class="src">
      Run against <a href="https://github.com/SalesforceFoundation/NPSP">NPSP</a> @ 19646faa,
      <code>force-app</code> subtree, with <code>fidelic/emulator:4ea9d49</code>. Three trigger
      rows are shown out of 26; the rest come out of the same command.
    </p>
    <div class="terminal" role="img" aria-label="Terminal transcript: the sfapex analyzer classifies the Nonprofit Success Pack's 26 triggers at load time as 7 EXECUTE and 19 UNRESOLVED; the emulator then boots that org in 14.1 seconds and, with failure injection enabled, a PATCH returns HTTP 400 with an UNABLE_TO_LOCK_ROW error array.">
      <pre><span class="dim"># what will run, before running it</span>
<span class="cmd">docker run --rm -v ./npsp/force-app:/mdapi:ro --entrypoint /sfapex fidelic/emulator coverage /mdapi</span>
COVERAGE
  parsed:   1061 files (0 with ERROR nodes, 0 error nodes total)
  found:    26 trigger(s), 1503 class(es)

RUNG B LADDER @ C1 + C2 families &#123;async, callout, crypto, database, recorded-effect, schema, schema-permission, settings&#125; <span class="dim">(load-time classification)</span>: EXECUTE=7  HOOKS=0  UNRESOLVED=19  RECORDED-ONLY=0  (SIMULATED=1)
  <span class="ok">EXECUTE</span>     TDTM_DataImportBatch  [Provisional: schema: describe labels derived where the metadata carries none; DisplayType modeled as its enum name; == identity on describe results Provisional (SCHEMA-DESIGN §7)]
  <span class="sim">EXECUTE*</span>    TDTM_FormTemplate  (SIMULATED: settings: Gift_Entry_Settings__c.getInstance — Custom Setting read at C2; settings: Gift_Entry_Settings__c.getOrgDefaults — Custom Setting read at C2)  [Provisional: schema: describe labels derived where the metadata carries none; DisplayType modeled as its enum name; == identity on describe results Provisional (SCHEMA-DESIGN §7)]
  <span class="bad">UNRESOLVED</span>  TDTM_HouseholdObject  (min tier C3; blocked by dispatch: interface Evaluator [C3])

<span class="dim"># now boot it, with the row-lock fault armed</span>
<span class="cmd">docker run -p 8080:8080 -v ./npsp/force-app:/mdapi:ro -e SFEMU_FAULT_LOCK_ON_UPDATE=true fidelic/emulator --apex-src /mdapi</span>
sfemu: apexexec ON — 26 triggers loaded from "/mdapi", 7 EXECUTE-classified
sfemu … listening on :8080 (store: sqlite ":memory:", client-secret: false, metadata: "/mdapi", hooks-file: "")
<span class="dim"># 14.1s to listening — NPSP, 26 triggers / 1,503 classes, 3-vCPU cloud VM</span>

<span class="cmd">curl -sX PATCH localhost:8080/services/data/v62.0/sobjects/Account/001000000000038AAA \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -d '&#123;"Rating":"Hot"&#125;'</span>
<span class="bad">HTTP 400</span>
<span class="bad">[&#123;"message":"unable to obtain exclusive access to this record or 1 records",</span>
<span class="bad">  "errorCode":"UNABLE_TO_LOCK_ROW","fields":[]&#125;]</span></pre>
    </div>

    <h2>Conformance — {data.conformance.passed}/{data.conformance.total}</h2>
    <p class="src">Produced by <code>{data.conformance.command}</code></p>
    <p>
      Every test is tied to Salesforce's own documentation or to an observation from a real org.
      A test with no source does not go in.
    </p>
    <p class="denom"><strong>Denominator:</strong> {data.conformance.denominator}</p>

    <h2>Classification over a real codebase — {data.coverage.percent}%</h2>
    <p class="src">Produced by <code>{data.coverage.command}</code> against {data.coverage.corpus}</p>
    <p>
      {data.coverage.executePairs} of {data.coverage.pairs} trigger-context pairs classify
      EXECUTE, across {data.coverage.executeTriggers} of {data.coverage.triggers} triggers. This
      is a public, real Salesforce codebase, not a fixture we chose.
    </p>
    <p class="denom"><strong>Denominator:</strong> {data.coverage.denominator}</p>
    <p>
      The unit is the <strong>pair</strong>, not the trigger. A trigger declaring seven timings
      and a one-line before-insert trigger are not the same amount of an org, so counting
      triggers makes the figure swing on which <em>size</em> of trigger happens to be blocked. On
      this corpus the two framings differ by three points in our favour, which is why the harder
      one is published.
    </p>

    <h2 id="vocabulary">The exact words</h2>
    <p>
      <a href="/capabilities">The capability page</a> uses three plain labels. Your own coverage
      report uses these, and so does the emulator's boot output.
    </p>
    <dl class="vocab">
      <dt><span class="v v-exec">EXECUTE</span></dt>
      <dd>Nothing in the reachable code is unsupported, so the interpreter will run your real Apex.</dd>
      <dt><span class="v v-sim">SIMULATED</span></dt>
      <dd>Your code runs but one or more named things were modelled rather than really done.</dd>
      <dt><span class="v v-prov">Provisional</span></dt>
      <dd>Faithful behaviour whose response <em>envelope</em> still awaits confirmation against a
      real org. A different axis from SIMULATED — a capability can be neither, either, or both.</dd>
      <dt><span class="v v-ref">UNRESOLVED</span></dt>
      <dd>Declined by name, with a reason and a file and line.</dd>
      <dt><span class="v v-rec">RECORDED-ONLY</span></dt>
      <dd>The code could run, but the event that starts it cannot happen locally: a
      platform-event subscriber, for instance.</dd>
    </dl>

    <h2>Every capability family</h2>
    <p class="src">
      Produced by <code>{data.capabilitiesCommand}</code>, read from the same maps the classifier
      consults at runtime — so a capability cannot ship without its label.
    </p>
    <p class="fine">
      These are the internal families the grouped table on <a href="/capabilities">the capability
      page</a> is folded from. They are our tier boundaries rather than Salesforce's vocabulary,
      which is why they are here and not there.
    </p>
    <div class="tablewrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Family</th><th>What it covers</th><th>Verdict</th><th>Caveat</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {data.capabilities.map((c) => (
            <tr>
              <td><code>{c.family}</code></td>
              <td>{c.note}</td>
              <td>
                <span class={c.verdict === 'EXECUTE' ? 'v v-exec' : 'v v-ref'}>{c.verdict}</span>
              </td>
              <td>
                {c.simulated && <div class="caveat"><span class="v v-sim">SIMULATED</span> {c.simulationNote}</div>}
                {c.provisional && <div class="caveat"><span class="v v-prov">Provisional</span> {c.provisional}</div>}
                {!c.simulated && !c.provisional && <span class="none">—</span>}
              </td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Run it against your own org</h2>
    <pre><code>docker run --rm -v "$PWD/mdapi":/mdapi:ro \
  --entrypoint /sfapex fidelic/emulator coverage --report /mdapi</code></pre>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .stamp{font-size:.85rem;color:#57606a;background:#f6f8fa;border-left:3px solid #89b4fa;
    padding:.6rem .85rem;border-radius:0 4px 4px 0}
  .src{font-size:.85rem;color:#57606a;margin-bottom:.4rem}
  .denom{font-size:.85rem;color:#24292f;background:#f6f8fa;padding:.6rem .85rem;border-radius:4px}
  .fine{font-size:.9rem;color:#57606a}
  .tablewrap{overflow-x:auto}
  table{border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;font-size:.9rem;margin:.5rem 0 1.5rem}
  th,td{text-align:left;padding:.45rem .6rem;border-bottom:1px solid #d0d7de;vertical-align:top}
  th{font-size:.8rem;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:.03em;color:#57606a}
  .v{display:inline-block;font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace;font-size:.72rem;
    padding:.08rem .4rem;border-radius:3px;white-space:nowrap;margin-right:.25rem}
  .v-exec{background:#dafbe1;color:#0a5a2a}
  .v-sim{background:#fff4d6;color:#7a5200}
  .v-prov{background:#e7e9ff;color:#33389a}
  .v-ref{background:#ffe9ec;color:#8b1d2c}
  .v-rec{background:#eef1f4;color:#44506b}
  .caveat{font-size:.82rem;color:#57606a;margin-bottom:.3rem}
  .caveat:last-child{margin-bottom:0}
  .none{color:#8c959f}
  .vocab dt{margin-top:.6rem}
  .vocab dd{margin:.15rem 0 .4rem 0;font-size:.92rem}
  @media (max-width:640px){ table{font-size:.82rem} }
</style>
